      },
      "reason": "The stage roster and the gate roster ARE the repo's identity — which converters exist and which gates it can currently pass — so two repos with different pipelines have different stage lists by definition. Measured 2026-08-21: strip the comments and the STEPS body and the driver is byte-identical, so all the divergence is in the two parts a shared table would have to hold. Canonical runs 22 stages to beta's 14 (24 until FORK-2 retired the two form-variant generators), not a subset either way (beta drives convert-archetype-inherents.cjs, which canonical has no copy of), and 15 post-loop gate blocks to beta's 7. Those blocks are not a stage list: they carry dataset predicates, existsSync optionality, a python3 runner, a ts-esm-register runner and two --dataset arg conventions, so a declarative table for them is a predicate/runner/optionality DSL written to describe a list of shell commands. And a shared table is a file canonical edits and beta EXECUTES at the head of the pipeline that produces beta's committed data, where a stage carrying the wrong repo-membership flag is silent on beta's side.",
